---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { findGuide, guideUrl } from '../../data/guides';
import { guideSchema } from '../../data/schema';

const guide = findGuide('hermes');
---

<Base
  title={guide.title}
  ogTitle={guide.h1}
  description={guide.description}
  canonical={guideUrl(guide)}
  schema={guideSchema(guide)}
>
  <section>
    <h1>{guide.h1}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hermes Agent starts each MCP server in its configuration as a local process and talks to it
      over stdio. agent-chaperone goes in front of that process, and a plugin puts the same screens
      in front of the tools Hermes runs itself: its terminal, its file edits and its web fetches.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page covers both. The plugin needs 0.4.0 or later, and so does <code>wrap</code> for
      Hermes's YAML configuration.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="install">
    <h2>
      Install it{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#install" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <pre><code>npm install -g agent-chaperone</code></pre>
    <p>
      A global install means Hermes starts the proxy directly, and it is where the plugin below
      finds the command and its own files.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="wrap">
    <h2>
      Put a server behind it{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#wrap" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <p>
      Hermes reads servers from <code>~/.hermes/config.yaml</code>, under <code>mcp_servers</code>.{' '}
      <code>agent-chaperone wrap</code> makes the edit. It prints what it would change and writes
      nothing until you add <code>--write</code>, keeps the original beside the file, and writes
      back only the entries it changed, so the comments in the file stay.
    </p>
    <pre><code>agent-chaperone wrap ~/.hermes/config.yaml
agent-chaperone wrap ~/.hermes/config.yaml --write</code></pre>
    <p>A server entry before and after:</p>
    <pre><code>{`mcp_servers:
  filesystem:
    command: npx
    args: ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/home/me/project"]`}</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{`mcp_servers:
  filesystem:
    command: agent-chaperone
    args:
      - --server
      - filesystem
      - --
      - npx
      - -y
      - "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem"
      - /home/me/project`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Everything after <code>--</code> is the server that would have run anyway.{' '}
      <code>--server</code> names the section of the policy file that applies, after the name you
      gave the server in Hermes. Keys such as <code>env</code>, <code>timeout</code> and{' '}
      <code>enabled</code> are kept as they were. <code>--unwrap</code> takes it all back out.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="key">
    <h2>
      Give it the API key{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#key" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <p>
      The model screens need <code>TYPESAFE_API_KEY</code>. Hermes does not hand a server its own
      environment: a stdio server gets a short list of safe variables, such as <code>PATH</code> and{' '}
      <code>HOME</code>, plus whatever the entry's <code>env</code> names. A key exported in your
      shell does not reach the proxy on its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      Put the key in <code>~/.hermes/.env</code> and name it in the entry. Hermes fills in{' '}
      <code>{'${...}'}</code> from that file when it starts the server, so the key itself stays out
      of <code>config.yaml</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`mcp_servers:
  filesystem:
    command: agent-chaperone
    args: ["--server", "filesystem", "--", "npx", "-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/home/me/project"]
    env:
      TYPESAFE_API_KEY: "\${TYPESAFE_API_KEY}"`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The proxy removes its own keys before it starts the server behind it, so the server never
      receives <code>TYPESAFE_API_KEY</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Without a key the proxy still starts, the deterministic rules still run, and every judgment
      records that no model was asked. It never refuses to start because a key is missing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="plugin">
    <h2>
      Screen Hermes's own tools{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#plugin" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <p>
      The terminal, file edits and web fetches Hermes runs itself never cross an MCP connection. A
      plugin reaches them. Hermes ignores what a shell hook prints after a tool has run, so only a
      plugin can change what the model reads, and this one hands each call and each result to{' '}
      <code>agent-chaperone hook pre</code> and <code>hook post</code>. It ships in the npm package:
    </p>
    <pre><code>cp -R "$(npm root -g)/agent-chaperone/integrations/hermes" ~/.hermes/plugins/agent-chaperone
hermes plugins enable agent-chaperone</code></pre>
    <p>
      Hermes loads no plugin until it is enabled, which is what the second line does. It adds the
      name to <code>plugins.enabled</code> in <code>config.yaml</code>, and you can write that by
      hand instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      A held call goes to Hermes's own approval prompt. Its "always" answer is remembered for that
      exact call, so it never allows every later use of the tool. Hermes approves the prompt by
      itself when <code>approvals.mode</code> is <code>off</code> or YOLO mode is on, and refuses
      the call when nobody is there to answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      A result judged unsafe is withheld: the plugin hands Hermes a notice in place of the tool's
      result. The tools it screens, and what each command answers, are in{' '}
      <a href="/docs/hooks#hermes-agent">the hooks reference</a>. MCP tools are left to the proxy,
      so a wrapped server is not screened twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plugin starts the command with Hermes's own environment, which includes{' '}
      <code>~/.hermes/.env</code>, so the key from the step above reaches it too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="check">
    <h2>
      Check that it is working{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#check" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <pre><code>hermes mcp test filesystem
hermes plugins list</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first connects to the wrapped server, which starts the proxy in front of it. If it fails,{' '}
      <code>hermes logs mcp</code> shows what the process printed. The second should show{' '}
      <code>agent-chaperone</code> as enabled.
    </p>
    <p>Then use the server and the terminal once from the agent, and read what was decided:</p>
    <pre><code>agent-chaperone log</code></pre>
    <p>
      Each line is one call or result, with the decision and, in shadow mode, what enforcing would
      have done instead. Calls from the plugin are recorded under the server name{' '}
      <code>built-in</code>. A line ending in <code>[not screened]</code> was decided without a
      model, which means the key did not arrive.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cannot">
    <h2>
      What this does not cover{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#cannot" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        <b>A live run, yet.</b> The plugin and the YAML support were written against Hermes Agent
        0.16.0's published contract and its source, and tested on payloads in its shape. They have
        not been checked in a live run of Hermes yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A result that is not a string.</b> A tool that returns images alongside text hands Hermes
        a structure rather than a string, and the plugin leaves it as it came, unscreened.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A failure of the plugin's own.</b> A missing <code>agent-chaperone</code>, a crash, or an
        answer that does not parse lets the call through rather than blocking it. The plugin waits
        25 seconds, under the 30 Hermes allows a plugin before it refuses the call itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A server that authenticates with headers.</b> Headers written in the Hermes entry stay
        with Hermes and never reach the proxy, which is why <code>wrap</code> leaves such an entry
        alone. Put it behind the proxy by hand, as{' '}
        <a href="/guides/mcp-security#wrap">the MCP guide</a> shows, and pass the token with{' '}
        <code>--header-env</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>What a server or a tool does rather than what the call says.</b> This is not a sandbox.
        It reads the call and the result, and a tool that does something its arguments did not
        describe is outside it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>
      Next{' '}
      <a class="anchor" href="#next" aria-label="Link to this section">
        #
      </a>
    </h2>
    <p>
      It starts in shadow mode, which blocks nothing.{' '}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-security">The MCP guide</a> covers what each screen asks, how to read a
      shadow-mode log, and when to switch to enforce. The exact wording of every question is in{' '}
      <a href="/docs/design">the design document</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
